"""
A release's changelog as structured data.

This is the contract between a producer that may vary between runs — a
language model, a command, a conventional-commit parser, or a person — and
the deterministic consumers that render it. Authoring may vary; rendering may
not. Every published artifact describing a release is rendered from one
instance, so no two artifacts can disagree.

A producer states the impact of each change through its entries' `kind` and
`breaking`, never the impact of the release: the release tooling suggests the
bump from those. Where a maintainer names the bump explicitly, the named bump
governs.
"""
struct Changelog {
    """
    Version of this schema that the instance conforms to.

    Consumers reject an instance declaring a schema version they do not
    implement rather than rendering it partially.
    """
    schema_version: String

    """
    Left unset by every producer and ignored by the tooling, which suggests
    the bump from the entries' kinds and breaking flags.
    """
    proposed_bump: Bump?

    """
    The resolved version this changelog describes, without a tag prefix — for
    example `1.4.0`.

    Left unset while a changelog is being authored, and filled in once the
    version is resolved. Renderers require it, because a rendered section is
    headed by the version it describes.
    """
    version: String?

    """
    A short headline for the release, at most one sentence.

    Rendered above the entries where a target supports it. Omitted for
    releases that carry no theme worth naming — most patch releases.
    """
    title: String?

    """
    One or two paragraphs introducing the release in prose.

    This is where a release earns readable notes: what changed in aggregate,
    who should care, and what to do about it. Entries carry the itemized
    record; this carries the narrative.
    """
    summary: String?

    """
    The itemized record of what changed, in the order it should be rendered.

    Renderers group entries by `kind` and preserve this order within each
    group, so authoring order is presentation order.

    A breaking change is an entry with `breaking` set, carrying its own
    `migration`. There is no separate collection for them: a release-level
    breaking note is an entry whose sources span the changes that caused it.
    """
    entries: [ChangelogEntry]

    """
    Every adapter that contributed material to this corpus, and the commit
    range each read.

    Carried from the evidence the corpus was authored from, so a reviewer can
    tell what the prose was written from. Empty for a corpus written by hand.
    """
    sources: [AdapterRun]

    """
    The commits no entry cites, each with why.

    Every commit in the evidence is either cited by an entry or listed here.
    Never rendered.
    """
    omitted: [OmittedCommit]?
}

"""
A commit that no entry cites.
"""
struct OmittedCommit {
    """
    The full commit hash, as the evidence carries it.
    """
    hash: String

    """
    One line saying why no entry cites the commit.
    """
    reason: String
}

"""
One adapter's contribution to a corpus: which adapter, and the range it read.
"""
struct AdapterRun {
    """
    The adapter that contributed.
    """
    adapter: Adapter

    """
    The full hash of the range's base commit: the commit the previous release
    was tagged on. Absent for a release line with no release yet, whose range
    starts at its root.
    """
    from: String?

    """
    The full hash of the range's head commit: the newest commit the adapter
    read.
    """
    to: String

    """
    The diff budget report of the evidence the corpus was authored from. Set
    on the run of the `DIFFS` adapter.
    """
    budget: BudgetReport?
}

"""
A source of changelog material.
"""
enum Adapter {
    """
    Commit records read from git: subjects, bodies, trailers and the pull
    request numbers in squash subjects.
    """
    COMMITS

    """
    Pull request titles and descriptions fetched from the hosting platform.
    """
    PULL_REQUESTS

    """
    The changes each commit made, read from git.
    """
    DIFFS
}

"""
A single itemized change.
"""
struct ChangelogEntry {
    """
    What kind of change this is. Determines which section renders it.

    Every entry a model or a person authors carries one. Only the mechanical
    producer writes an entry with none, for a commit whose subject carries no
    conventional type; such an entry renders under the other heading.
    """
    kind: ChangeKind?

    """
    One or two full sentences stating the change from the reader's point of
    view.

    Written as an outcome a consumer can recognize, not as the work that
    produced it: "closed decoders reject undeclared keys", not "refactored the
    decoder".
    """
    summary: String

    """
    The part of the product this change affects, in the project's own
    vocabulary — for example `transpilers/go` or `cli`.

    The same notion as a conventional-commit scope. Rendered as a prefix
    where a target supports it, and unset where a change is not scoped to one
    area.
    """
    scope: String?

    """
    Additional prose for a change that cannot be understood from its summary
    alone.

    Rendered beneath the summary. Most entries need none.
    """
    detail: String?

    """
    Whether a consumer that upgrades is broken by this change.
    """
    breaking: Bool

    """
    What a consumer must do to adopt this change, where anything is required.

    Required where `breaking` is set: a breaking change without migration
    guidance is an incident report rather than a release note. Also used
    where adoption is optional but non-obvious.
    """
    migration: String?

    """
    Where this change came from — the pull requests, commits and issues that
    together account for it.

    Rendered as links where the target supports it. An entry may cite several
    sources of different kinds, which is how one entry describes work that
    arrived across more than one pull request.
    """
    sources: [ChangeSource]

    """
    Whether `summary` was derived mechanically from a commit subject rather
    than written by a person or a model.

    Set only by the mechanical producer, for a summary it derived from a
    commit subject: nobody wrote the sentence.
    """
    derived: Bool
}

"""
A reference to something that accounts for a change.
"""
union ChangeSource {
    """
    A pull request the change arrived through.
    """
    PullRequest: PullRequestSource

    """
    A commit the change arrived through, for work that reached the release
    outside a pull request.
    """
    Commit: CommitSource

    """
    An issue or work item the change resolves.
    """
    Issue: IssueSource
}

"""
A pull request that carried a change.
"""
struct PullRequestSource {
    """
    The pull request number, as the hosting platform assigns it.
    """
    number: Int
}

"""
A commit that carried a change.
"""
struct CommitSource {
    """
    The full commit hash. Renderers abbreviate it for display.
    """
    hash: String
}

"""
An issue or work item a change resolves.
"""
struct IssueSource {
    """
    The identifier in the tracker's own form — for example `strata-8da` or
    `412`.
    """
    id: String
}

"""
The release impact of a change or of a release as a whole.
"""
enum Bump {
    """
    Breaks a consumer that upgrades without changes.
    """
    MAJOR

    """
    Adds capability while remaining compatible.
    """
    MINOR

    """
    Corrects behaviour without adding capability.
    """
    PATCH

    """
    Nothing a consumer of the published artifacts would observe.
    """
    NONE
}

"""
The category of an itemized change, which decides the section it renders in.

The variants correspond one-to-one with the conventional-commit types, spelled
out rather than abbreviated, so a parser reading conventional commits maps onto
them mechanically while a producer writing prose reads a vocabulary that needs
no decoding. Whether a change is breaking is
carried by `ChangelogEntry.breaking` rather than by a variant, matching
conventional commits' treatment of it as a marker rather than a type.

A project typically renders `FEATURE`, `FIX`, `PERFORMANCE` and `REVERT`, and
omits the rest from published notes.
"""
enum ChangeKind {
    """
    A new capability a consumer can use.
    """
    FEATURE

    """
    A correction to behaviour that was wrong.
    """
    FIX

    """
    A change whose observable effect is speed or resource use.
    """
    PERFORMANCE

    """
    A change that reverts earlier work.
    """
    REVERT

    """
    A change to behaviour-preserving structure.
    """
    REFACTOR

    """
    A documentation change.
    """
    DOCUMENTATION

    """
    A change to the build system or to dependencies.
    """
    BUILD

    """
    A change to continuous integration configuration.
    """
    CI

    """
    A change to tests.
    """
    TEST

    """
    A formatting or style change with no behavioural effect.
    """
    STYLE

    """
    Maintenance work that fits no other category.
    """
    CHORE
}
